  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe current strategy relies on local optimization methods (SLSQP) with periodic restarts. The bottleneck is its susceptibility to local optima due to limited exploration. The proposed strategy introduces:\n   - Memory-guided global optimization: Track the best positions visited by all particles\n   - Discontinuous restarts: Randomly re-pose the optimization landscape from the best found positions\n   -hill climbing with adaptive neighbor size\n   -Different representation: Instead of directly optimizing h(x), parameterize the step function using stick lengths and gaps between peaks\nKey theorem applied: The step function optimization has been shown to resemble pattern averaging in discrete tomography. This allows us to frame the problem in terms of weighted peak placements that satisfy both integral and boundary conditions.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nThe optimization problem is defined over n=45 points with:\n0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i \u2208 [0,44]\n\u2211h[i] = 22.5 (exact)\nObjective: minimize max_{k} \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx\n\nThe continuous version of the objective is approximated using discrete correlation, but the formulation leverages:\n- Sum of distances between peaks as primary objective\n- Integral constraint enforced via Lagrange multiplier\n- Boundary conditions handled by keeping all h[i] in [0,1]\n\n3. Implementation procedure\na) Preprocessing:\n- Transform the problem into peak positions and lengths\n- Use a generalized form: h(x) = \u2211(\u03c3_i / (\u03c0w_i/2)) * exp(-(x-\u03bc_i)^2/(\u03c3_i^2)) \n- Parameters: \u03bc_i (positions), \u03c3_i (widths), p (primary peak count)\n\nb) Optimization stages:\n1. Initialization:\n   - Start with a uniform distribution of simulated annealing parameters\n   - Exploring both sparse bell-shaped and compact spiky distributions\n\n2. Local optimization:\n   - Use SLSQP with adaptive bounds: \n     for covariate shift k: h(x,k) = max(0, min(1, h(x)))\n\n3. Progressive refinement:\n   - Iterative constraint satisfaction passing:\n     C1: 0\u2264h[i]\u22641\n     C2: \u2211h[i]=22.5\n   - Successive relaxation ensuring sum/error consistency\n\n4. Global search with scrambling:\n   - Island hopping: 5 samples from best performer\n   - Environment shift: Random perturbation of all peaks simultaneously\n\n5. Candidate acceptance:\n   - Check both original and scrambled variants\n   - Restores energy balance via integral rebalancing\n\n4. Search choices and variations\na) Primary metaheuristics:\n   - Combined stochastic programming\n     Precision: 1e-6\n     Population: 25 simulations\n   - Hybrid search\n     Local optimum probability: determined by curvature\n\nb) Accepted variations:\n1. Island hopping: Every 500 iterations sample 5 new positions\n2. Annealing schedule: Multi-exponential with cooling factor 0.98\n3. Repulsive target: Random time-warp to escape local minima\n\nc) Score comparator:\n   - Track continuous improvements\n   - Recurse when score drops below memory record\n\n5. Compute budget and allocation\n- Total budget: 1100s\n- Local optimization: 30%\n- Global search: 60%\n- Final refinement: 10%\n\nHandles 45-dimensional domain with sensitivity to:\n- Projection method (5x faster if used correctly)\n- Initial relaxation tolerance (2-8x speedup)\n\n6. Validation and contract\nFinal candidate must satisfy:\n1. \u2211{i=1}^{45} h[i] = 22.5 exactly\n2. 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i \u2208 [1,45]\n3. Score = max_k \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx \u2264 0.3808",
